VERDICT: OVERRULE
CHECKER: judge-standards
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: W4
ATTEMPT: 4
---
The contested FAIL is technically accurate about the sparkline (confirmed:
`git diff master -- web/static/js/charts.js` is empty — the file is
byte-identical to master, not touched by any commit on this branch;
`internal/services/metrics/metrics_test.go:920` does assert
CombinedCumulativeBalance[-1] == -CombinedCumulativeDelta, "within
month-rounding slack"). But standards-conformance is not just "is the
technical claim true" — it is "does this claim, as scoped, control
acceptance of THIS attempt." It does not, on the documents as written.

1. Two rulings are in tension, and the more specific/later one governs.
   Ruling 2026-08-29a states a general principle at attempt-2 time ("a
   threshold applied to a figure must live in ONE classification source
   consumed by every surface that renders the same figure"). Ruling
   2026-08-29c is the user's own, later, task-specific reopening of W4 for
   attempt 4, and it is drawn narrowly and explicitly: "the Budget card's
   container tint AND headline consume the shared BudgetVerdict
   classification (closing the last two independent classifiers of
   CombinedCumulativeDelta — an inconsistency that pre-exists on master) +
   the five kpis.html trim markers. Nothing else." The ruling-writer
   explicitly flags "pre-exists on master" while still bounding the reopen
   to two named classifiers — i.e., the user was already aware pre-existing
   inconsistencies of this general shape exist elsewhere and chose "nothing
   else" anyway. A later, task-specific, user-authored ruling narrowing an
   escalated attempt's scope controls over a general principle stated two
   attempts earlier; ordinary reading of the documents does not let 29a's
   language silently re-widen a scope 29c just closed with "Nothing else."

2. The checker's hunting brief expands what gets discovered/reported, not
   the acceptance bar. checker-second's own account says it was "instructed
   to hunt for any surface still classifying the same deltas independently"
   — a reasonable adversarial instruction for the FAMILY: adversarial lane.
   But its sibling checkers, tasked with the same attempt, drew the correct
   line: checker-tests found a criterion ("zero numeric comparison literals
   remain") that literally could not be satisfied without breaching 29c's
   "Nothing else," and explicitly deferred — "I read the ruling as
   controlling and record the criterion met" — flagging the tension for the
   lead rather than failing on it. checker-a11y found several pre-existing,
   untouched-by-this-diff contrast issues and correctly filed them as
   non-blocking OBSERVATIONS rather than FAILs, on the same reasoning: "Same
   class strings before and after this diff." checker-second's own hunt
   should have produced the same disposition — a flagged observation for
   the lead, not a FAIL — because the item found is (a) not in the
   `.swarm/manifests/W4.4.files` manifest, (b) unmodified by any commit on
   `fix/phase-visibility` relative to master, (c) a different rendered
   figure (CombinedCumulativeBalance, target-actual) from the one 29c named
   (CombinedCumulativeDelta, actual-target), related only via a codebase
   invariant test carrying admitted "month-rounding slack," and (d)
   something the user's own ruling already flagged as a known,
   deliberately-deferred pre-existing condition.

3. The hard-stop rule's stated purpose is that "escalation never silently
   loops." W4 already burned a Tier-3 escalation and a user-authorized,
   narrowly-bounded fourth attempt. Treating "any same-figure surface
   anywhere on the page, including pre-existing ones outside the manifest
   and outside every prior ruling's enumeration" as the acceptance bar for
   attempt 4 means no fourth attempt at the scope the user actually
   authorized could ever pass — each pass would surface a new pre-existing
   sibling classifier (the chart today, something else next time), exactly
   the silent, unbounded loop the hard-stop exists to prevent. The proper
   channel for a genuinely new, out-of-scope finding at this stage is a
   reported observation triggering a fresh, separately-scoped task/ruling —
   not a FAIL that re-litigates a scope the user already closed.

The FAIL does not survive scrutiny against the documents as written: it
applies a superseded general ruling to override a later, explicit,
task-specific "Nothing else" bound, on an item outside the attempt's
manifest and untouched by any commit in this run. OVERRULE.
